  <section id="how-to-read">
    <h2>How to read this page</h2>
    <p>
      The article is a general history of Circassians in Syria and mentions Amin
      Samkough in only two short paragraphs. It is also someone else’s published
      work, so I have <strong>not</strong> translated it in full. This page
      summarizes the article in my own words and translates only the two
      sentences about Samkough. Please have a Russian reader check the
      translations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of,
      or a place where this article disagrees with other sources on this site.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="overview">
    <h2>What the article covers</h2>
    <p>In order, the article describes (my summary):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Circassian pashas.</strong> The close ties between Circassia and
        the Ottoman Empire, and the many Circassians (and Abkhazians and
        Georgians) who became Ottoman governors and commanders. It counts the
        governors of Damascus between 1516 and 1758 by origin, and tells the
        story of a fearless 17th-century Circassian pasha described by the
        Ottoman traveller Evliya Çelebi.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Circassian muhajirs.</strong> How the empire’s defeat by Russia
        led to the resettlement of Circassians in Syria and elsewhere in Asia
        after the Treaty of Berlin (1878), and what the Ottoman authorities
        hoped to gain by settling them there. It says more than 45,000
        Circassian refugees from European Turkey were moved to Syria, with
        settlements around Aleppo, the Golan Heights, Amman–Balqa and the Tiberias
        region.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Circassians and Druze.</strong> Violent clashes in Lebanon in
        1894 and 1895.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Travellers’ accounts.</strong> What Laurence Oliphant (who
        visited Circassian settlers in Quneitra), the German archaeologist
        Schumacher and Gertrude Bell wrote about Circassians, and how many held
        senior offices in the late Ottoman provinces.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The French mandate.</strong> The recruitment of Circassian
        cavalry into the French “Special Troops”, the Circassian national
        movement, the school in Quneitra, and the Second World War and
        independence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>After 1946.</strong> Circassians in the Syrian army and politics
        in the 1950s and 1960s, the 1967 war and the displacement from the Golan,
        and the Circassian Charitable Society founded in Damascus in 1948.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="samkough">
    <h2>What it says about Amin Samkough</h2>
    <p>
      He appears in two paragraphs in the section on the French mandate. The
      first covers the society, its publications and the newspaper:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="ru">
        Группа активистов во главе с Амином Самгугом в 1927 г. основала в
        Кунейтре «Черкесское общество образования и единения»…
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          “A group of activists headed by Amin Samgug founded the ‘Circassian
          Society for Education and Unity’ in Quneitra in 1927…”
        </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The article goes on to say the society produced pamphlets and school
      textbooks in Circassian using a Latin-based alphabet, and that the
      newspaper “Mardj” carried articles in Circassian, Arabic, Turkish and
      French. It gives no dates for the newspaper.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second paragraph is about the school. It doesn’t name Samkough, but it
      follows directly after the passage about him:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="ru">
        В 1932 г. была открыта черкесская десятилетняя школа в Кунейтре…
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          “In 1932 a ten-year Circassian school was opened in Quneitra…”
        </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      It adds that the school was meant for children from the whole district, and
      that alongside general subjects taught in Arabic, the curriculum included
      Circassian language, folk customs, songs and dances.
    </p>
    <p>That is everything the article says that is directly about him.</p>

    <h3>Things to review</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>not an independent source.</strong> The wording about
          the society, the newspaper and the school closely matches the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/kushkhabiev-circassian-community-in-syria/">
            Kushkhabiev article
          </a>
          (the same 1927 society name, the same four newspaper languages and
          the same “ten-year school” in 1932). It was probably based on that
          work, so please don’t count it as a second confirmation.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the school’s opening year.</strong> This article says
          1932. The Mirzoev article and the biography page say 1933.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the society’s name.</strong> “Society for Education
          and Unity” here, but “Society for the Promotion of Education and
          Culture” in the Mirzoev article, which describes the first as the
          literal translation from Circassian.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the newspaper’s languages.</strong> It says four
          languages, but the front page of issue 71 (August 1931) on the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/marj-newspaper/">Marj page</a> is in
          Ottoman Turkish and Arabic, with no Circassian text on it.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the name of the newspaper.</strong> “Mardj” here, but
          “Marj” and “Marge” elsewhere. These are transliterations of the same
          Arabic-script name.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Related context to review</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the article says Ahmad Nami, a Circassian and son-in-law of
          Sultan Abdul Hamid II, was appointed president of the Syrian state in
          <strong>1925</strong>. The Kushkhabiev article and the list of Syrian
          presidents I checked give 28 April 1926. The date may be an error in
          this article.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the numbers (such as the tally of Damascus governors, or
          45,000 refugees resettled in Syria) are the author’s claims. I
          haven’t checked them.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
